{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for task/fresh-session-orientation at exact head bc806522f62fe5609942b7ab3d15f5820ed74675. Head and clean status verified before and after; probes deleted, worktree unchanged. Gates green at this head: vet, build, test, -race. Disclosure: a delegate ran the sweep under my direction; I reproduced both leading findings myself before signing.\n\nThe bridge commit is genuinely empty and correctly so: bc806522^{tree} and 681cc0d^{tree} are both 7d8678d7fa6a1a2fc650541edd4d903da292972e. It carries no code, only a Rests-On trailer re-anchoring the already-final tree onto a later act. I verified the tree equality rather than take it on the message.\n\nF1, BLOCKER. resident_cached overrides local projection meaning, which is the one condition I said I would lean hardest on. orientationCompatible checks genesis, fingerprint and projection version, the caller adds head equality, and residentWhoami then republishes orientation.Durable and orientation.Depth wholesale. Three of those four are free for any answerer: the fingerprint is echoed back from the path the client itself requested, and the head is a public git ref in a repository the answerer can read. Nothing constrains the two fields that carry meaning. I built a stub resident that returns the client's own genesis, its own fingerprint, the correct projection version and the real local head, and fabricates the rest. whoami answered source resident_cached with no degraded marker, durable name \"impostor\", roles sequencer, operator and ratifier, membership_event \"fabricated\", and frontier depth 31337 against a real depth of 1 and a real name of \"human\". \"fabricated\" is not even an event-id shape. A cache answer changed what the projection means, silently, which is precisely the defect class the conditions name. Close it by binding the meaning-bearing fields to something locally checkable: MembershipEvent has the form git:\u003cfmt\u003e:\u003cgenesis\u003e#git:\u003cfmt\u003e:\u003ccommit\u003e, so the client can confirm in O(1) and with no fold that its genesis component equals Config.Genesis and that the commit object exists locally, and the same for RoleSources entries. Depth is fully determined by genesis plus head, so either verify it or stop republishing a resident-supplied number as the client's own frontier.\n\nF2, MUST FIX. The redirect policy does not pin the configured origin. newResidentClient's CheckRedirect calls validateResidentURL on the redirect target, which asks only whether it is some loopback http or https URL, and never compares it to the configured baseURL. I confirmed this through the shipped client rather than a test-injected one: a server on the configured port redirected to a different loopback port and the client followed it and accepted the answer. Off-loopback redirects are correctly refused, so the escape is lateral rather than outward, and on its own it needs control of the configured resident — but combined with F1 it means --server names a port rather than a peer, while its own error text promises \"a trusted local cache\". Aggravating: every whoami test injects httpServer.Client() or resident.Client(), so the shipped policy is never exercised on the orientation path at all. The resident API defines no redirects; refuse them outright, and add one whoami-level test that uses newResidentClient().\n\nF3, MUST FIX. A resident whose head never converges permanently suppresses degraded. frontierMoved is retryCompatible \u0026\u0026 (retry.Head != orientation.Head || retryHead != localHead), and both disjuncts can be satisfied by the resident alone, so a resident returning a fresh head on every probe reports local_full_audit_frontier_moved with no degraded flag, indefinitely. The audit data stays honest; only the health signal is falsified. Suppress degraded only when the resident's head matched some locally observed head during the window, which is what a genuinely straddled append looks like and what a flapping resident cannot produce.\n\nF4, MUST FIX, tests. Ten load-bearing guards were removed or weakened one at a time and the suite stayed green for five of them, including the genesis check, the fingerprint check, the 64KiB bound both removed and weakened a thousandfold, warm-before-listen, Cache-Control no-store, the headErr == nil gate, the orientation timeout, and — worst — the retry leg's head agreement, which is the single most meaning-critical line in that path. Probes were then written for five and each fails under its mutation and passes unmutated, so these are genuine coverage gaps rather than artifacts of the mutation choices. The guards that ARE covered are covered well: projection version, first-probe head, off-loopback redirect, credentials and query and fragment, retry removal, 404, server version, projection leak, and all three fallback assertions.\n\nF5, MUST FIX. Three tests cannot fail. The 64KiB bound test writes oversized non-JSON, so with the bound removed the unbounded decoder hits a syntax error and falls back anyway — the fixture must be valid, compatible, oversized JSON. The no-local-replay test calls Snapshot on the resident handle immediately after service.New, warming the workspace itself and masking the guard it appears to cover. The stall test asserts elapsed \u003e 3*orientationTimeout, a threshold defined in terms of the constant under test, so raising the constant raises the bar with it.\n\nF6, fix. whoami emits the actor struct, which carries KeyFile, so the reply contains the absolute path of the actor's private key. Pre-existing, but this commit makes whoami the documented orientation surface and path leakage is an explicit condition. The /v0/orientation response itself is clean.\n\nWHAT HELD, and the benchmark repair is real. The honest-fallback requirement survived every hostile resident built against it — dead server, connection reset mid-response, truncated JSON, null, wrong types, HTML, empty 200, 404, 500, foreign genesis, foreign fingerprint, missing projection version, stale head, thirty-second stall, oversized — all reaching offline_full_audit with degraded true and a locally audited durable identity. The 64KiB bound holds against a gzip bomb, since the limit sits on the decompressed stream, and against Content-Length understated and overstated; a resident streaming forever is cut off at 1.16s. Warm-before-listen is material. Unknown fingerprint returns 404 with no-store. The projection never leaves via orientation. Fold verification is config-independent, so same genesis plus same head really does imply same fold for an honest resident, and the Snapshot cache is keyed on the ref, so Head and Durable always come from one coherent fold — F1 is about dishonest answerers, not incoherent ones. On methodology, the sibling branch's defect is NOT present here: the benchmark builds depth with real signed Act calls, and re-measurement shows the cold arm linear in depth at roughly 86ms per event while the warm arm stays flat, which is exactly the evidence a padded-map fixture cannot produce. That repair is genuine. One nit: the benchmark never asserts the achieved depth, so it would silently degrade to depth 1 if Act ever began deduplicating."}